@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+---
+id: dl-007-applymergepatch-follows-rfc-7386-strictly-null-removes-a-member-objects-merge-recursively-any-other-value-replaces
+type: decision-log
+title: "applyMergePatch follows RFC 7386 strictly: null removes a member, objects merge recursively, any other value replaces"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Some clients send partial updates in JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386). The library is imported by other team
+projects, so `applyMergePatch` must follow the standard exactly, as `applyPatch` does for RFC 6902 (dl-005).
+RFC 7386 defines a small algorithm, and its examples in Appendix A show the cases a looser implementation
+gets wrong: a `null` member removes a member, an array replaces rather than merges, and a non-object patch
+replaces the whole target.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` follows the RFC 7386 algorithm:
+  - if the patch is not an object (including an array, a string, a number, a boolean or `null`), the
+    result is the patch itself;
+  - otherwise, if the target is not an object (an array counts as not an object), the target is treated
+    as `{}`;
+  - each member of the patch is then applied in patch order: a member whose value is `null` removes the
+    member of the same name from the result, if there is one, and does not create it if there is not;
+    any other member is merged recursively into the member of the same name, or into `undefined` when the
+    result has no such own member.
+- Arrays are values, not merged: an array in the patch replaces the target member, and `null` elements
+  inside an array are kept, because only object members are removed.
+- Objects are recognised as plain records (`typeof` `object`, not `null`, not an array). Member lookup uses
+  own properties only, so an inherited name such as `constructor` is not a member of the target.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Other projects get the standard's behaviour and can rely on it, including the Appendix A examples.
+- A `null` in a patch cannot store a JSON `null` in an object. To set a member to `null`, a client must
+  send the whole object instead. This is inherent to RFC 7386, and is not a library limit.
+- Because a patch is applied as a whole, a patch member that is an object merges into a target object of
+  the same name, not replaces it. A caller that wants replacement must send `null` first, then the value,
+  or use `applyPatch` (RFC 6902) instead.
