@@ -0,0 +1,32 @@
+---
+id: dl-008-applymergepatch-returns-a-new-document-never-mutates-its-inputs-and-does-not-throw
+type: decision-log
+title: "applyMergePatch returns a new document, never mutates its inputs, and does not throw"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+RFC 7386 describes the merge as an algorithm that changes `target` in place, and it defines no error
+cases: every JSON value is a valid merge patch. The library already has the same concern for `applyPatch`,
+where the input document is often shared with other code that still reads it (dl-003), and where failures
+are reported by throwing (dl-004). `applyMergePatch` must fit the same conventions.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` never modifies `target` or `patch`. It makes one deep copy of `target`
+  up front, merges into that copy, and returns the result, or a copy of `patch` when the patch replaces the
+  whole document. Values taken from the patch are copied, so the result shares no object with the patch.
+- `applyMergePatch` does not throw for a JSON patch: the RFC defines no error, so there is no invalid patch
+  to report. Any value of the `patch` argument is accepted, as RFC 7386 requires.
+- Data that is not JSON (for example a `Date`, or a function) is copied by `structuredClone` as it is, as
+  `applyPatch` does today. Whether it should be rejected is left to the decision on plain JSON data (dl-006),
+  which concerns `applyPatch` and will be applied to this function if it is approved.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The caller's target and patch are never mutated, and the caller can rely on that, as with `applyPatch`.
+- Each call copies the whole target, which costs time and memory on large documents (as in dl-003).
+- Callers do not need a `try`/`catch` for a merge. A non-JSON value inside the target, such as a
+  function, makes `structuredClone` throw, as it does for `applyPatch`.
